```{r setup, include=FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  fig.width = 10,
  fig.height = 7,
  dpi = 110,
  fig.cap = NA,
  fig.path = "figs/",
  out.width = "100%"
)
# Figures (Plan 04 PERF-12): ragg for cleaner text, an opaque white
# background so the PNG needs no alpha channel, and lossless optipng
# recompression. vignettes/precompute.R refuses to run without optipng.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(dev = "ragg_png", dev.args = list(background = "white"))
knitr::knit_hooks$set(optipng = knitr::hook_optipng)
knitr::opts_chunk$set(optipng = "-o5 -strip all")
```

```{r imports, include=TRUE, message=FALSE}
library(gtropic)
```

`gtropic_plot()` turns a `gtropic_data()` result into static exposure, count
and track maps, or interactive exposure and count maps. It never downloads
data or recomputes published values.

## A single storm

Look Hurricane Katrina up by name, then retrieve it by its unique `STORM_ID`
(a name such as `"Katrina-2005"` can match more than one storm):

```{r katrina-query}
katrina_id <- gtropic_storms(STORM_NAME = "Katrina-2005")$STORM_ID
katrina <- gtropic_data(STORM_ID = katrina_id, date_range = 2005)
```

With the default 500 km radii and windows of two days before to one day after
closest approach, the result holds the units within 500 km of Katrina's track.

### Exposure maps

The default is an exposure map with a panel for each core metric: maximum
sustained wind (`VMAX_SUST_MS`), total precipitation (`PRECIP_MM`) and
largest flooded share (`PROP_FLOODED_AREA`). The caption records the radii and
windows of the plotted data.

```{r katrina-plot}
gtropic_plot(katrina)
```

`gtropic_metrics()` lists every metric that can be mapped and how it is
aggregated per unit by default:

```{r}
gtropic_metrics()
```

`aggregate` overrides the default, for example total against largest daily
precipitation:

```{r}
gtropic_plot(katrina, metric = "PRECIP_MM", aggregate = "sum")
```

```{r}
gtropic_plot(katrina, metric = "PRECIP_MM", aggregate = "max")
```

`metric` chooses which exposures to show, one panel each:

```{r}
gtropic_plot(
  katrina,
  metric = c("VMAX_SUST_MS", "VMAX_GUST_MS", "WIND_RADII_VMAX_SUST_MS")
)
```

```{r}
gtropic_plot(
  katrina,
  metric = c("SUST_DUR_MINUTES", "GUST_DUR_MINUTES", "WIND_RADII_SUST_DUR_MINUTES")
)
```

`metric`, `aggregate`, `palette` and `direction` take vectors aligned by
position, so each panel keeps its own scale. `?gtropic_plot` lists the other
settings, such as `basemap`:

```{r multi-metric-map}
gtropic_plot(
  katrina,
  metric = c("VMAX_SUST_MS", "PRECIP_MM"),
  aggregate = c("max", "sum"),
  palette = c("inferno", "mako"),
  track = FALSE,
  basemap = "admin1"
)
```

### Track maps

`type = "track"` draws only the storm track. Points are coloured by the
Saffir-Simpson categories of `gtropic_saffir_simpson()`, in metres per second;
`track_map_key` takes another key, and `track_interval` shows points every
three or six hours while the path keeps every observation:

```{r}
gtropic_plot(katrina, type = "track", basemap = "admin1", track_interval = 3)
```

### Categorical maps

`wind_categories` sorts units into classes by the wind they received, using a
key like the one `gtropic_saffir_simpson()` returns:

```{r}
gtropic_saffir_simpson()
```

```{r}
gtropic_plot(katrina, wind_categories = gtropic_saffir_simpson())
```

The Saffir-Simpson key is allowed only for `VMAX_SUST_MS` with
`aggregate = "max"`, where it describes local sustained wind, not storm
status. Other metrics and aggregates need a custom key with `category`,
`threshold`, `unit` and `color` columns. Categories must be unique, thresholds
are increasing upper bounds ending in `Inf` (a value equal to a threshold
belongs to that category), and `unit` is one value matching the metric's unit
(`"m/s"` and `"meters per second"` both work). With several metrics, pass a
list of keys named by metric. For example, two classes:

```{r}
two_class_wind <- data.frame(
  category = c("Below hurricane", "Hurricane-force"),
  threshold = c(32, Inf),
  unit = "m/s",
  color = c("#9ECAE1", "#DE2D26")
)

gtropic_plot(
  katrina,
  metric = "VMAX_SUST_MS",
  wind_categories = two_class_wind,
  track = FALSE
)
```

### Interactive maps

`interactive = TRUE` returns a Leaflet exposure or count map. It uses vector
layers and needs no tile-server key.

```{r interactive-map, eval=FALSE}
gtropic_plot(katrina, interactive = TRUE, basemap = "none")
```

## Several storms

The storms of 2019 that came within 500 km of a unit in India:

```{r}
india_19 <- gtropic_data(WHO_ENTITY = "India", date_range = 2019)

india_19$storm_metadata
```

```{r}
gtropic_plot(india_19, type = "track")
```

The largest sustained wind each unit received from any of them:

```{r}
gtropic_plot(india_19, metric = "VMAX_SUST_MS", track_labels = FALSE)
```

Without the tracks:

```{r}
gtropic_plot(india_19, metric = "VMAX_SUST_MS", track = FALSE)
```

`bbox` zooms in on a region:

```{r}
gtropic_plot(
  india_19,
  metric = "VMAX_SUST_MS",
  track = FALSE,
  bbox = c(xmin = 82.5, ymin = 17.5, xmax = 87.7, ymax = 25),
  expand = 0
)
```

### Count maps

Count maps count, per unit, the storms or local days on which a metric met its
threshold. The caption records the definitions used.

```{r count-map}
gtropic_plot(india_19, type = "count", count_unit = "days", track = FALSE)
```

```{r}
gtropic_plot(india_19, type = "count", count_unit = "storms", track = FALSE)
```

`threshold` changes the definitions:

```{r}
gtropic_plot(
  india_19,
  type = "count",
  metric = c("VMAX_SUST_MS", "PRECIP_MM"),
  count_unit = "days",
  threshold = list(VMAX_SUST_MS = 0, PRECIP_MM = 10),
  track = FALSE
)
```

## Saving plots

Static maps are ggplot objects: add layers, then save them with
`gtropic_save()`.

```{r save-plot, eval=FALSE}
p <- gtropic_plot(katrina, metric = "VMAX_SUST_MS", basemap = "none") +
  theme_gtropic(base_size = 11)

gtropic_save(p, "katrina-wind-exposure.png", dpi = 300)
```

## Summary

- `gtropic_plot()` draws exposure, count and track maps from a `gtropic_data`
  object; exposure and count maps can also be interactive.
- Metrics, aggregation, category keys, thresholds and the map extent make the
  displayed exposure definition explicit, and the caption records it.
- Static maps can be extended with ggplot2 and saved with `gtropic_save()`.

